Rapid prototyping is a design philosophy made to establish direction for development. 
With an alpha, a team figures out all the places the final game is going to 
need assets by making a version with placeholders in the alpha. 
From here, a team assigns the tasks of creating the individual assets to 
its members and implementing the assets in the beta and polishing them in the gold.

The point of the Alpha stage is forethought. 
Bottlenecks mostly happen by accident and coincidence, 
so thoroughly planning development is the best means to prevent them. 
Knowing what tasks depend on others helps the team plan the order that tasks are to be done in. 
The thing that sets a bottleneck apart from a simple dependency 
is that a bottleneck prevents other work from getting done because the work left to be done depends on tasks that are being worked on. 
The alpha helps a team set a plan to get dependent tasks out of the way early. 
Thorough planning through the alpha is how my team will reduce bottlenecking.

The beta stage serves as a goal to work towards. 
Most of development will be spent getting the game to the beta, where it is playable, but needs refinements. 

The gold is the ultimate goal, a polished game.
